#doc (Manual) "Introduction" =>
%%%
tag := "sec:intro"
%%%

Theorem A is about homomorphisms of multiplicative groups; Theorems B, C and D, about rational
Milnor $`K`-theory, are consequences of it.
Each theorem is followed by its formal statement in Lean; {ref "ss:reading"}[Section 9.2]
explains how to read these statements.

# Statement for multiplicative groups
%%%
tag := "ss:statementA"
%%%

Let $`L` be a field and $`l \subset L` a relatively algebraically closed subfield.
Put
$$`V_{L|l} = (L^\times / l^\times) \otimes_\ZZ \QQ,`
a $`\QQ`-vector space which we write additively; the class of $`a \in L^\times` is denoted
$`[a]`.
Every non-zero $`v \in V_{L|l}` satisfies $`Nv = [a]` for some integer $`N \geq 1` and some
$`a \in L \sm l`.
Elements $`v_1, \dots, v_m` of $`V_{L|l}` are called _algebraically independent over_ $`l` if
the corresponding elements $`a_1, \dots, a_m` of $`L` are; this does not depend on the choices.
The group $`V_{L|l}` does not change if $`L` and $`l` are replaced by their perfect closures
$`L^i` and $`l^i`.

:::thm "A" (tag := "thm:A")
Let $`K` and $`L` be fields, $`k \subset K` a subfield, $`l \subset L` a relatively
algebraically closed subfield, and let $`\psi \colon K^\times \to V_{L|l}` be a homomorphism such
that

* *(A1)* $`\ker \psi = k^\times`;
* *(A2)* for every $`x \in K \sm k`, the elements $`\psi(x)` and $`\psi(1-x)` are algebraically
  dependent over $`l`;
* *(A3)* there are $`x_1, \dots, x_d \in K` with $`\psi(x_1), \dots, \psi(x_d)` algebraically
  independent over $`l`, where $`d = 2` if $`\chr L = 0` and $`d = 3` if $`\chr L > 0`;
* *(A4)* if $`\chr L > 0`, then $`\chr K > 0`.

Then $`\chr K = \chr L`, the field $`k` is relatively algebraically closed in $`K`, and there are
a field embedding $`\tau \colon K \to L^i` with $`\tau^{-1}(l^i) = k` and a number
$`r \in \QQ^\times` such that
$$`\psi(x) = r \cdot [\tau(x)] \qquad \text{for all } x \in K^\times.`
If $`(\tau', r')` is another such pair, then $`\tau' = \Frob^j \circ \tau` and
$`r' = p^{-j} r` for some integer $`j`, where $`p` is the characteristic exponent; in
characteristic $`0` the pair is unique.
:::

In Lean, Theorem A is stated three times: for a perfect field $`L` (then $`L^i = L` and
$`l^i = l`), for an arbitrary field $`L`, and, separately, the uniqueness clause.
In all three, $`K` is an arbitrary field.

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.theoremA_perfect}

The hypotheses keep their names.
The homomorphism $`\psi` is an additive homomorphism `Additive Kˣ →+ V l L`, and
`ψ (.ofMul x)` is $`\psi(x)`.
Hypothesis (A2) quantifies over pairs of units `x`, `y` with `x + y = 1`, so that $`1 - x` is
never a junk value, and "algebraically dependent" is the negation of
{name}`VAlgIndep`.
In (A3) the number $`d` is `if ringChar L = 0 then 2 else 3`; Lean's `ringChar` is $`0` in
characteristic $`0`.

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.theoremA_perfect_unique}

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.theoremA}

Here $`L^i` is any field $`M` which is a perfect closure of $`L` in the sense of Mathlib's
`IsPerfectClosure`, and $`l^i` is the subfield {name}`perfectClosureIn` of elements
of $`M` some $`p^n`-th power of which lies in $`l`.
The comparison map {name}`V.toPerfectClosure` is the identification
$`V_{L|l} = V_{L^i|l^i}` ({ref "ss:perfectClosure"}[Section 2.3.1]).

Conversely, every pair $`(\tau, r)` as in the theorem defines a homomorphism
$`\psi = r[\tau]` satisfying (A1) and (A2).

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.theoremA_converse}

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.theoremA_converse_perfectClosure}

The hypotheses of Theorem A can be satisfied together: if a perfect field $`L` contains $`d`
elements algebraically independent over $`l`, then $`K = L`, $`k = l` and $`\psi = [\,\cdot\,]`
satisfy (A1)–(A4), and $`L = \QQ(X_0, X_1)` is an explicit example.

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.theoremA_perfect_hypotheses}

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.theoremA_perfect_hypotheses_ratFunc}

For function fields of transcendence degree at least $`2` over algebraically closed fields, a
statement of this kind is due to {citet Bib.BT}[].
Theorem A has no assumption on the fields, and its proof is different.

# Statements for Milnor K-theory
%%%
tag := "ss:statementsK"
%%%

For a field $`K` let $`\KM_*(K)` be its Milnor ring, the quotient of the tensor algebra of
$`K^\times` by the ideal generated by the elements $`x \otimes (1 - x)`, and put
$`\Kr_*(K) = \QQ \otimes \KM_*(K)`.
For a subfield $`k \subset K` let $`\Kr_*(K|k)` be the quotient of $`\Kr_*(K)` by the ideal
generated by $`k^\times \subset \Kr_1(K)`; if $`k` is relatively algebraically closed, then
$`\Kr_1(K|k) = V_{K|k}`.
A field embedding $`\tau \colon K \to L` with $`\tau(k) \subset l` induces
$`\tau_* \colon \Kr_*(K|k) \to \Kr_*(L|l)`, and for $`r \in \QQ^\times` we write $`r^{\deg}` for
the automorphism of a graded $`\QQ`-algebra which is multiplication by $`r^n` in degree $`n`.
To keep the statements simple, the fields in Theorems B–D are perfect; this is harmless, because
$`\Kr_*` does not change under purely inseparable extensions.

In Lean, $`\Kr_*(L|l)` is {name}`MilnorKQ` `(constUnits l L)`, the tensor algebra
over $`\QQ` of $`V_{L|l}` modulo the Steinberg relations, and $`\Kr_*(L)` is
`MilnorKQ (⊥ : Subgroup Lˣ)`, the same construction with $`L^\times \otimes \QQ` in place of
$`V_{L|l}`.
A homomorphism of graded algebras is an algebra homomorphism `φ` with
{name}`MilnorKQ.IsGraded` `φ`, and $`\varphi_1` is
{name}`MilnorKQ.degOne` `φ`.
These definitions are discussed at the beginning of {ref "sec:K"}[Chapter 7].

:::thm "B" (title := "Relative version") (tag := "thm:B")
Let $`K, L` be perfect fields with relatively algebraically closed subfields $`k, l`, and let
$`\varphi \colon \Kr_*(K|k) \to \Kr_*(L|l)` be a homomorphism of graded $`\QQ`-algebras.
Assume that $`\varphi_1` is injective, that $`\psi = \varphi_1 \circ [\,\cdot\,]` satisfies (A3),
and that (A4) holds.
Then there are a field embedding $`\tau \colon K \to L` with $`\tau^{-1}(l) = k` and
$`r \in \QQ^\times` such that $`\varphi = r^{\deg} \circ \tau_*`.
The pair $`(\tau, r)` is unique up to $`(\Frob^j \circ \tau, p^{-j} r)`.
:::

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.theoremB}

The homomorphism $`\tau_*` needs a proof that $`\tau(k^\times) \subset l^\times`; it is produced
from `hτ`, inside the existential, by {name}`constUnits_le_comap`.

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.theoremB_unique}

:::thm "C" (title := "Absolute version") (tag := "thm:C")
Let $`K, L` be perfect fields, and let $`k_0, l_0` be the algebraic closures of the prime fields
in $`K` and $`L`.
Assume that $`K \neq k_0`.
Let $`\varphi \colon \Kr_*(K) \to \Kr_*(L)` be a homomorphism of graded $`\QQ`-algebras such that
$`\varphi_1` is injective, the composition $`K^\times \to \Kr_1(L) \to V_{L|l_0}` satisfies (A3),
and (A4) holds.
Then there are a field embedding $`\tau \colon K \to L` and $`r \in \QQ^\times` such that
$`\varphi = r^{\deg} \circ \tau_*`, and $`\tau^{-1}(l_0) = k_0`.
The pair $`(\tau, r)` is unique up to $`(\Frob^j \circ \tau, p^{-j} r)`.
:::

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.theoremC}

The subfields $`k_0` and $`l_0` are arguments of the theorem, characterized by the hypotheses
`hk₀` and `hl₀`; the prime field of $`K` is `⊥ : Subfield K`.
The projection $`\Kr_1(L) = L^\times \otimes \QQ \to V_{L|l_0}` is
{name}`VOf.proj`.

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.theoremC_unique}

:::thm "D" (title := "Isomorphisms") (tag := "thm:D")
Let $`K` and $`L` be fields each of which contains three elements that are algebraically
independent over the prime field; if one of them has characteristic $`0`, two such elements
suffice.
Then every isomorphism of graded $`\QQ`-algebras $`\varphi \colon \Kr_*(K) \to \Kr_*(L)` is of
the form $`r^{\deg} \circ \tau_*` for an isomorphism of fields $`\tau \colon K^i \to L^i` and
some $`r \in \QQ^\times`.
In particular $`K^i \cong L^i`.
:::

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.theoremD}

"If one of them has characteristic $`0`, two such elements suffice" is read as: both fields
contain $`d` algebraically independent elements, where $`d = 2` if one of the characteristics is
$`0` and $`d = 3` otherwise.
An isomorphism of graded algebras is an algebra isomorphism `φ` which is graded (`hφ`).
Its inverse is then graded too ({name}`MilnorKQ.IsGraded.symm`), because the Milnor ring is the
direct sum of its graded pieces ({name}`MilnorKQ.gradedAlgebra`).

For transcendence degree at least $`5`, Theorem D and the analogue of Theorem B for
isomorphisms were proved by {citet Bib.T}[]; the passage from $`K`-theory to fields there
goes through the classification of "alternating pairs" by valuations and through the
reconstruction of a field from its lattice of relatively algebraically closed subfields.
The present approach avoids both: the only input from $`K`-theory is the Steinberg relation
$`\{x, 1 - x\} = 0` and one family of homomorphisms out of $`K_2`, the tame symbols of
{cref "lem:tame"}[].

# The idea
%%%
tag := "ss:idea"
%%%

In characteristic $`0` the proof of {cref "thm:A"}[] is short.
The logarithmic derivative is an injective $`\QQ`-linear map
$`\dlog \colon V_{L|l} \to \Omega_{L|l}`, and two elements of $`V_{L|l}` are algebraically
dependent if and only if their images are proportional.
So $`\Delta = \dlog \circ \psi` is a homomorphism $`K^\times \to \Omega_{L|l}` with
$`\Delta(1-x) = \mu(x) \Delta(x)` for a function $`\mu` with values in $`L^\times`.
The identity
$$`1 - xy = (1 - x)(1 - u), \qquad u = \frac{x(1-y)}{x-1},`
applied to two elements with linearly independent $`\Delta(x), \Delta(y)`, gives a functional
equation for $`\mu` which says precisely that
$$`t(x) = \frac{\mu(x)}{\mu(x) - 1}`
is multiplicative; and $`t(1-x) = 1 - t(x)` holds for formal reasons.
Hence $`t` is a field embedding $`\tau`, and a short argument identifies $`\psi` with a rational
multiple of $`[\tau]`.

The identity is checked by Lean (hover over the code for types and documentation):

```lean
example {K : Type} [Field K] (x y : K) (hx : x ≠ 1) :
    1 - x * y = (1 - x) * (1 - x * (1 - y) / (x - 1)) := by
  have : x - 1 ≠ 0 := sub_ne_zero.mpr hx
  field_simp
  ring
```

The same two lines prove it inside {name}`StepBData.Transverse.t_mul`, the formal
version of {cref "lem:twovar"}[].

In characteristic $`p` the group $`V_{L|l}` is $`p`-divisible and has no logarithmic derivative.
One has to choose a non-perfect _model_ $`L_A \subset L` and use $`\Omega_{L_A|l}`, which only
sees the elements of the correct Frobenius _level_.
The whole difficulty is to show that $`\psi(x)` and $`\psi(1-x)` have the same level
({cref "prop:levels"}[]).
This is where three independent elements and hypothesis (A4) are used: a configuration of seven
points shows that the level discrepancy is a homomorphism $`K^\times \to \ZZ`, which must vanish
because $`K^\times` is divisible by the characteristic.
Once this is known, the characteristic $`0` computation runs in the model.

A reader interested only in characteristic $`0` can skip {ref "sec:levels"}[Chapters 3–5],
{cref "sec:stepA"}[] and the parts of {ref "sec:main"}[Chapter 6] marked "characteristic p",
read $`L_A = L` everywhere, and ignore all statements about levels, heights and Frobenius.
This is also how the formalization is organized: the characteristic $`0` case of Theorem A is a
separate proof ({name}`theoremA_perfect_of_charZero`), and the two cases share only
the standard facts of {ref "sec:conv"}[Chapter 2] and the abstract form of Step B
({ref "ss:stepB"}[Sections 6.4] and {ref "ss:embedding"}[6.5]).

# What is used
%%%
tag := "ss:used"
%%%

Besides linear algebra, the proof uses Kähler differentials of separably generated field
extensions, Gauss' lemma and unique factorization in polynomial rings, the norm of a finite
separable extension, and, for the $`K`-theoretic statements only, the existence of an extension
of a valuation to a larger field.
No result about Milnor $`K`-theory beyond its definition is used.

Gauss' lemma, unique factorization and the norm are needed only in positive characteristic
({cref "lem:sepcrit"}[] and {cref "prop:rational"}[]); the formalization does not use Lemma 3.1,
which it replaces by a criterion with differentials ({cref "ss:sepcrit"}[]).
The blueprint also uses Gauss' lemma in the proof of the eigenvalue lemma ({cref "lem:R"}[]) and a
discrete valuation in the proof of (S3) ({cref "lem:standard"}[]); the formal proofs avoid both.
The formalization takes from Mathlib: Kähler differentials (`KaehlerDifferential`) with the
base change theorem for formally étale extensions, separability and perfect closures
(`IsPerfectClosure`), algebraic independence and the algebraic matroid, and valuation subrings
with Chevalley's extension theorem (`Ideal.image_subset_nonunits_valuationSubring`).

# Status
%%%
tag := "ss:status"
%%%

The blueprint is an unrefereed working draft.
The statements go beyond the literature cited (in the transcendence degree, and in allowing
non-surjective homomorphisms), so it should be read as a detailed proposal for a proof.
Its characteristic $`0` part and its Section 7 are now machine-checked; the positive
characteristic part is being formalized.

The main theorems depend on exactly one unproved declaration,
{name}`theoremA_core_charP`: Theorem A for perfect fields $`K` and $`L` with
$`\chr L > 0`, whose proof is the subject of {ref "sec:levels"}[Chapters 3–5] and of the
characteristic $`p` parts of {ref "sec:main"}[Chapter 6].
Its proof is `sorry`, and at the time of writing (November 2026) it is the only `sorry` in the
library.
The formalization of Chapters 3–5 (in `MilnorAlgDep/CharP/`) has just been merged; Steps A
and C in positive characteristic are in progress.
